-- title:
--   Retraction kernel detects the ∞-component: wₚj-jᵖ∈𝔭
-- statement:
--   Fix a positive integer $N$ and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid N$, and write $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbf{Q}$ consisting of the rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$. For a level $M$, `modularFunctionFieldFull M` is the subfield of $\mathbf{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbf{Q}$ by the series $j(q^d)$ for the positive divisors $d$ of $M$, and `chartAlgFin M p` is the $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$-subalgebra of elements of that field which are integral over $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}[j]$; `jChartFin M p` denotes $j$ as an element of it. Let $\kappa$ be a field of characteristic $p$ that is a $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$-algebra. The data are: a $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$-algebra map $\iota \colon$ `chartAlgFin N p` $\to$ `chartAlgFin (N*p) p` which preserves Laurent series, i.e. $\iota(b)$ and $b$ have the same $q$-expansion; a $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$-algebra automorphism $w$ of `chartAlgFin (N*p) p` inducing on `modularFunctionFieldFull (N*p)` the automorphism `atkinLehnerInvolutionFull N p` (the chosen $\mathbf{Q}$-automorphism interchanging $j(q^d)$ and $j(q^{dp})$ for all positive $d \mid N$, if such exists, and the identity otherwise); a $\kappa$-algebra map $\sigma_0 \colon \kappa \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}}$ `chartAlgFin (N*p) p` $\to \kappa \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}_{(p)}}$ `chartAlgFin N p` with $\sigma_0 \circ (\mathrm{id}_\kappa \otimes \iota) = \mathrm{id}$; and an ideal $\mathfrak{p}$ of `chartAlgFin (N*p) p` that is a minimal prime over the ideal generated by $p$. The assertion is that the ideal generated by the image of $\mathfrak{p}$ under $b \mapsto 1 \otimes b$ is contained in $\ker \sigma_0$ if and only if $w(\iota(j)) - \iota(j)^p \in \mathfrak{p}$.
--
--   The ideal $(p)$ in the $j$-finite chart ring of the integral model of $X_0(Np)$ has the two minimal primes corresponding to the two components of the Deligne–Rapoport reduction of $X_0(Np)$ modulo $p$, and Ogg's function $w_p j - j^p$ vanishes exactly on the component through the cusp $\infty$; the statement says that a retraction of the base-changed degeneracy map $\iota$ can only kill the component on which $\iota$ becomes an isomorphism. It is used in the analysis of sections of the two-chart Igusa model over $\kappa$ and in the evaluation of such a retraction on modular units.
